WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.240
Welcome back to our second drink of Did I Stutter?

2
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:10.120
I wanted to kick us off, Jenna, because we got a lot of mail about this episode.

3
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.080
Many people wrote in to say that the phrase, did I stutter, is offensive and should not be used.

4
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.860
Some people said that while they could see a buffoon like Michael Scott using this phrase,

5
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.340
they were surprised that Stanley used it, and it's the title of the episode.

6
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:29.380
Yeah.

7
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.600
I mean, I think that's all valid.

8
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.160
Yeah, I do too.

9
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.620
It's definitely an outdated phrase for sure.

10
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:44.600
And, you know, it makes me think of something else from my childhood that people also used to do if you couldn't hear them.

11
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.740
Like, if they said something and you said what?

12
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:48.320
Okay.

13
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:48.980
Right?

14
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.540
Because that's what happens in the episode.

15
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:50.840
Yeah.

16
00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.220
And then Stanley's like, did I stutter?

17
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:53.580
Right.

18
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:59.200
Another thing that someone might do is sort of, like, mimic the vocal pattern of a deaf person,

19
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.040
like, to mock you for not being able to hear them or whatever.

20
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:02.580
Right.

21
00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.040
Well, you know, my sister wears hearing aids.

22
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.800
So did my grandfather and my aunt and uncle were deaf.

23
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.640
And it always kind of, like, I don't know, I didn't like it.

24
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.000
Right.

25
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.500
It's like it's making this—it's making a joke, making light of this.

26
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.000
Yeah.

27
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.280
I mean, it just—I don't know.

28
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.620
But that is something that we have phased out as well now.

29
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.200
And I'm glad we have.

30
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:26.600
Yeah.

31
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.000
And also good that we have phased out this phrase.

32
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.940
Because I don't hear people say that anymore.

33
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:33.960
I don't either.

34
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.700
And I'm really glad everyone wrote in.

35
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:36.300
Thank you, guys.

36
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:37.140
Yeah, thank you.

37
00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:44.060
Well, listen, Brent Forrester and Leslie David Baker gave us some great behind-the-scenes details about this episode.

38
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.220
But, Angela, I believe you found more?

39
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:47.240
I did.

40
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.360
So here's the thing.

41
00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.800
I went digging into the candy bag alts for this one.

42
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:57.100
And when we first broke down the episode, we talked about Michael's talking head at the very end of the episode.

43
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.780
You know, him and Stanley, they've kind of come to some type of an agreement.

44
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.040
And then Michael has this hilarious talking head where he does an impression of Rodney Dangerfield.

45
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.560
He actually does a bunch of impressions.

46
00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:08.960
Yes.

47
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.140
And I shared that it wasn't in the shooting draft.

48
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:12.840
Right.

49
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:18.540
You found the shooting draft in your digital clutter, and you wondered if there'd been candy bag alts for that.

50
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:18.940
Yeah.

51
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.520
But this was before we had access to all the candy bag alts.

52
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:23.240
Exactly.

53
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.260
So, yes, there are a bunch of candy bag alts.

54
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.160
So you were right.

55
00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.500
Your instincts were correct.

56
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.060
And I thought I'd read you one.

57
00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:31.600
Great.

58
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:31.980
Okay.

59
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.680
This one kind of made me sad.

60
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:34.480
Oh, no.

61
00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:34.760
Okay.

62
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.160
It's sad Michael.

63
00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.140
It's sad Michael.

64
00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:44.200
Michael says, I have a lot of friends, and I have a lot of employees, and they're the same people, mostly.

65
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:50.080
So if I lose an employee, I am also losing a friend, and I don't want to lose two friends.

66
00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:51.340
Oh.

67
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.080
Oh, sad Michael.

68
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.300
But, yes, there were lots of candy bag alts.

69
00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:04.980
Most of them are along the same vein of, like, him just trying to perform for the camera and thinking he's really hilarious.

70
00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.960
But I thought that one was, like, aw.

71
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.000
Well, we also got a fun catch from Taylor in Vancouver, British Columbia.

72
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.460
Taylor said, I noticed something.

73
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:21.060
It's not an old tech alert, per se, but it is a, quote, out-of-date thing, I suppose.

74
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:27.780
When Kevin asked Pam to say, quote, those will be due back on Wednesday because she is wearing glasses,

75
00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:34.840
I noticed that I haven't had an actual person say when my library books are due back in so long.

76
00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.220
It's a machine that does the book checkouts now, at least at my library.

77
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:40.880
That's so true.

78
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.000
Yes, I volunteered at the library, you know, at the elementary school when my kids were little.

79
00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:49.340
This was a very coveted volunteer spot, by the way.

80
00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:55.620
There was only two parents that could volunteer, and I snagged one of the spots, and I coveted it.

81
00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.020
Matt, I believe you are the library volunteer at your kids' school.

82
00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:00.620
Is this correct?

83
00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:01.220
Oh, yeah.

84
00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.360
I've been waiting years to become the library volunteer.

85
00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:04.580
Oh, my gosh, Matt.

86
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:05.680
It's such a big deal.

87
00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:06.820
Yeah, it's a good gig.

88
00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.660
So you have to pay your dues.

89
00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:09.440
You have to pay your dues.

90
00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.620
You have to kind of lobby for it.

91
00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.420
I didn't get it until fourth grade.

92
00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:13.360
I don't know about you.

93
00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.780
Well, I'm with my youngest, and she's in kindergarten,

94
00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.360
but that took a few years of, like, you know, campaigning with the older kids.

95
00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.540
So, yeah, no, I'm in now.

96
00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:21.420
You're in early.

97
00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.420
I went every Thursday, and I was there for story time, and I also helped restock the books.

98
00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:31.140
And I would scan books in for kids when they came in to return something, you know?

99
00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:31.600
Mm-hmm.

100
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:32.820
And it is.

101
00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.280
It has a little barcode, and you have, like, the little bing, you know,

102
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:38.360
little zappy thing.

103
00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:38.880
What do you call it?

104
00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.440
But you wouldn't remind people when their books were due verbally?

105
00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.000
You get an email, lady.

106
00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:43.580
Oh.

107
00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:44.680
You get an email.

108
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.020
So now these are kids in elementary school.

109
00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.080
So, Matt, I don't know how you guys are doing it,

110
00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.880
but they would get an email from the libraries, you know,

111
00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.260
at blah, blah, blah library school, right?

112
00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:55.640
Mm-hmm.

113
00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:00.820
That would just say, your child has a book that has been out for three months.

114
00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:05.080
It's on arachnids, and it's due back or something.

115
00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:09.520
I feel like Taylor in Vancouver is saying that there's not even a person there,

116
00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.260
that it's almost like a self-checkout, like the grocery store.

117
00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:13.640
Well, this is at school.

118
00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:14.140
Yes.

119
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.780
So, you know, kids need a person.

120
00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.040
But I'm sure all of your notifications are you.

121
00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:18.220
Oh, yeah.

122
00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.480
They scan their card, and it pops up.

123
00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.180
And if they have—I'm with the kindergartners, so if they have two books at home,

124
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.140
I say, sorry, Joshua, you can't check out a book this week.

125
00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.020
Please go sit down.

126
00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:28.380
You have to say no to the little kids.

127
00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:28.700
Oh, I love it.

128
00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:29.620
That's my favorite part.

129
00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:30.480
That's my favorite part.

130
00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:31.980
What is happening?

131
00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:33.220
Teaching them responsibility.

132
00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:34.100
Oh, my gosh.

133
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:34.420
Oh, I'm going to learn.

134
00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:35.740
I was such a softie.

135
00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:37.760
I'd be like, okay, you're not supposed to check any more out,

136
00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.600
but I'm going to let you have this one, but your parents are going to get an email.

137
00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.020
I say it very kindly, but I do, like, you know.

138
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:43.860
Yeah.

139
00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.000
I mean, that's part of the job.

140
00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.680
It might be why you secured the job, Matt.

141
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:47.360
I think so.

142
00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:47.840
Yeah.

143
00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.220
You can be the heavy.

144
00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:52.920
I also, Matt, oddly enjoyed restocking books.

145
00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:54.360
Did you enjoy that?

146
00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:56.640
Is that part of your job description?

147
00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:57.260
It is.

148
00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.880
I prefer the scanning and beeping and interacting with the children,

149
00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.120
but it is kind of Zen.

150
00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:03.360
You learn the Dewey Decibel.

151
00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.200
You do, and it took me a while.

152
00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:08.320
I'm like, oh, and then by the end, I had the layout of the library,

153
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:09.240
and I knew where everything was.

154
00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:10.780
It was a very coveted job.

155
00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:12.020
I loved that question.

156
00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:13.200
It kind of brought me right back.

157
00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.760
Well, listen, everybody, thank you for writing in,

158
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.040
and now here's your second drink of this episode.

159
00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.940
Oh, and teaser, y'all, Jenna has a fantastic deep dive on Mad Libs in this one.

160
00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:24.820
You won't want to miss.

161
00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:30.110
I'm Jenna Fisher.

162
00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:31.390
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

163
00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:32.670
We were on The Office together.

164
00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:33.930
And we're best friends.

165
00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:37.750
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

166
00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:42.130
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

167
00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:44.730
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

168
00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:45.890
We're The Office ladies.

169
00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:53.350
Oh, Angela, we have a good episode today.

170
00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.610
It is so good.

171
00:06:56.030 --> 00:07:03.450
It's so simple, so wonderful, so much tension, conflict, a little bit of drama.

172
00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.410
It's all there, and some laughs.

173
00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.570
It is called Did I Stutter?

174
00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:14.470
It is season four, episode 16, written by Brent Forrester and Justin Spitzer,

175
00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:17.290
and directed by Randall Einhorn.

176
00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:18.890
We've got a lot to cover.

177
00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:20.850
I say get into that summary, lady.

178
00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:21.890
Here it is.

179
00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:27.690
Stanley challenges Michael's authority during a conference room meeting, leaving Michael

180
00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:28.330
speechless.

181
00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:29.230
Mm-hmm.

182
00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:35.470
Later, Michael responds to Stanley's insubordination by attempting to scare him into an attitude

183
00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:37.430
adjustment with a fake firing.

184
00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.650
What is with Michael and the fake firings?

185
00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:41.650
I don't know.

186
00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.230
He thinks it's like a go-to.

187
00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:45.490
I mean, it did not go well with Pam.

188
00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:49.490
No, he should have abandoned this whole idea back then.

189
00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:49.930
Yeah.

190
00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:51.650
But he's still doing it.

191
00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:57.770
Meanwhile, Dwight decides to buy Andy's car at a discount, only to immediately flip it

192
00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:58.630
for more money.

193
00:07:58.870 --> 00:07:59.290
Mm-hmm.

194
00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:00.390
So shrewd.

195
00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:01.630
Did he shrewd it?

196
00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.330
He shrewded it.

197
00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:10.970
Pam has to deal with the unexpected inconvenience of wearing her backup glasses to work after spending

198
00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:13.190
the night at a friend's house.

199
00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:16.230
Her backup glasses from junior high?

200
00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:17.290
Perhaps.

201
00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:21.430
I'll have a lot to share about that.

202
00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:22.170
I bet.

203
00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:22.590
I bet.

204
00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:23.490
All right.

205
00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:24.970
Fast fact number one.

206
00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:28.990
We're going to talk a little bit about the inspiration for this episode.

207
00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:32.590
We had a fan question from Bianca O and Katie W.

208
00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:37.470
They wanted to know who came up with the idea for Stanley to sass Michael.

209
00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:39.110
It was me.

210
00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:40.130
No, I'm kidding.

211
00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:42.390
Well, Angela, you do love a sass.

212
00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:43.850
I love the sass.

213
00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:49.370
Well, we traded messages with writer Brent Forrester about this episode.

214
00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:55.990
And he told us he remembered being very excited when Greg assigned them the script because it

215
00:08:55.990 --> 00:09:00.130
was the biggest Stanley story he had ever done at that point.

216
00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:05.950
It was really the first time that Stanley had ever been the sole antagonist in a Michael story.

217
00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:11.510
I remember we were all so excited for Leslie just as actors to be able to have that big

218
00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:14.710
moment with Michael and to go head to head with Steve.

219
00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.170
That was like you hoped for that, right?

220
00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:18.330
Oh, yeah.

221
00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:19.470
Yeah, this was huge.

222
00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:25.890
Brent remembers Greg told him with great delight what the game of this episode was.

223
00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:27.610
It should be this.

224
00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:33.130
Michael is an overpowering boss to everyone in the office, but is totally unable to stand

225
00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.150
up to one person, Stanley.

226
00:09:35.650 --> 00:09:38.090
No one ever dares to challenge Michael this blatantly.

227
00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:42.770
So when Stanley snaps at him, Michael just has no playbook for dealing with it.

228
00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:48.650
He said that Mindy referred to this as reversal of status comedy.

229
00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:52.170
And it was the first time Brent had ever heard that term.

230
00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:54.250
Mindy, you're so smart.

231
00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:55.870
I know, right?

232
00:09:55.870 --> 00:10:00.430
I remember doing Commedia dell'arte in theater school, Angela.

233
00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:01.870
No, here we go.

234
00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:02.970
Did you ever do Commedia?

235
00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:04.110
No, Jenna.

236
00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.270
I did my improv and my stand up and my sketch comedy.

237
00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:08.970
So just let's hear it.

238
00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:15.430
Well, it's really very interesting because Commedia dates back to long, long ago, and it is really

239
00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:16.850
all about status.

240
00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:21.890
And you're constantly switching status between these archetypal characters.

241
00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:27.770
And this has some real Commedia elements for all you theater nerds out there, this reversal

242
00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:28.850
of status comedy.

243
00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:30.330
No, it's fantastic.

244
00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:35.410
I mean, even in improv, we would make someone, when you walk on stage, choose status, high

245
00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:35.770
or low.

246
00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:41.690
And if you constantly played high status, because some people, it's their go-to in comedy, our

247
00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:43.950
teacher would make you reverse it and play low status.

248
00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:45.670
It was always a really cool exercise.

249
00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:47.230
Okay, getting a little nerdy.

250
00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:48.730
Okay, we can move on.

251
00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:51.830
No, I love that we're nerding out on status comedy.

252
00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:55.930
Guys, watch comedy shows and look for status and reversal of status.

253
00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:56.930
That's your homework.

