538
00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:26.250
It is fascinating.

539
00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:28.430
Well, our kids love Mad Libs.

540
00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:29.850
I mean, Jenna, I did them growing up.

541
00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:30.790
We still do them.

542
00:24:31.130 --> 00:24:31.630
Same.

543
00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:33.910
Let me tell you the history of Mad Libs.

544
00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:34.310
Okay.

545
00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:41.810
Mad Libs was invented in 1953 by two men, Leonard Stern and Roger Price.

546
00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:45.790
Roger Price had worked as a writer on The Bob Hope Show.

547
00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:49.670
Leonard Stern was his friend who worked as a writer on The Honeymooners.

548
00:24:49.670 --> 00:24:51.210
They were comedy writers.

549
00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:59.170
One day, Roger went to visit Leonard at work, and Leonard was working on a script for The Honeymooners.

550
00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:05.830
He was trying to figure out the right word to describe the nose of Ralph Cramden's new boss.

551
00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:12.010
Before he could explain the whole thing, he just said to his friend, I need an adjective.

552
00:25:12.430 --> 00:25:16.590
And Price jumped in and started throwing out words like clumsy, naked.

553
00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:18.350
He just started throwing out adjectives.

554
00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:21.310
So, they thought this was so funny, right?

555
00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:24.210
Like, oh, a clumsy nose, a naked nose.

556
00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:28.750
So, this gave them the idea to write a bunch of stories with missing words.

557
00:25:29.030 --> 00:25:31.770
They took them to a cocktail party with their friends.

558
00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:35.010
They had people fill in the blanks without knowing the context.

559
00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:37.190
And it was a huge hit.

560
00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:38.910
People thought this was a hoot.

561
00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:42.650
They tried to get these stories published, but publishers passed.

562
00:25:42.830 --> 00:25:43.550
Nobody wanted them.

563
00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:45.090
They said, we don't get it.

564
00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:47.530
Maybe sell it to a game company or something.

565
00:25:47.690 --> 00:25:48.850
It sounds like a party game.

566
00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:52.010
So, they sat on the idea for five years.

567
00:25:52.810 --> 00:25:55.110
And then they decided to self-publish.

568
00:25:55.390 --> 00:25:57.770
But now they needed a name for it, right?

569
00:25:57.930 --> 00:25:58.070
Yeah.

570
00:25:58.150 --> 00:25:59.170
They had all their stories.

571
00:25:59.390 --> 00:26:00.310
They're self-publishing.

572
00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:01.430
Get this.

573
00:26:01.710 --> 00:26:04.550
These two guys, they're out having breakfast in New York.

574
00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:09.390
And they overheard an agent arguing with his client at another table.

575
00:26:09.830 --> 00:26:13.930
And the agent was trying to tell this actor that he had to recite a certain script

576
00:26:13.930 --> 00:26:15.530
for an upcoming interview.

577
00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:19.410
And the actor was saying, no, I want to ad-lib my interview.

578
00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:20.690
I want to ad-lib it.

579
00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:24.750
And the agent said, that would be mad to ad-lib an interview.

580
00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:27.670
And that's where the name Mad Lib came from.

581
00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:33.750
And now they have sold over 110 million copies of Mad Lib's books.

582
00:26:33.970 --> 00:26:35.410
That was amazing.

583
00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:37.930
I loved every single moment of that.

584
00:26:37.930 --> 00:26:43.410
But I also find it really inspiring that you can have this once-in-a-lifetime idea

585
00:26:43.990 --> 00:26:47.530
and, you know, don't let go of it, even if it takes you six years,

586
00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:49.990
to see it come, like, to fruition.

587
00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:50.970
Yeah.

588
00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:54.090
They were on to something and they knew it and they made it happen.

589
00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:55.070
I love that.

590
00:26:55.210 --> 00:26:55.990
Great deep dive.

591
00:26:56.450 --> 00:26:56.850
